item.description:
NORMALLY: Javascript accesses Supabases data for most things (lower level information called Row-Level Security)Some actions are too dangerous or privileged to let the browser do directly, like deleting someones login, or resetting someone elses password. If that function lived in the browser itself, anyone could call that function. When called, the function runs somewhere you dont control or expose, and it does the senstivie part with elevated credentials. The browser never sees or holds that elevated credential, it just requests the function gets run, and the function itself decides if thats allowed.Think of it as a locked door between your app and the dangerous stuff. The browser can knock and make a request; only the function on the other side has the key.This function is called when a new account is created.
Parameters taken: { name, email, password, role, departments }
Creates a new Supabase Auth login and inserts the matching profiles row and department access into the database. The browser could never call this function on its own.This is used to update permissions, update email, and name on the accounts. As the name suggests, this is the function for changing peoples passwords.
Parameters:{ userId, newPassword }
This deletes users outright. The frontend reauthenticates the admins password to verify the deletion should actually happen, THEN the function gets sent over to the edge function to irreversably delete the account.
Parameters:{ userId }
This function is less about privalages and security, and is more about working around a browser bug. Without this edge function, the browser builds and downloads the tracker files with no filename. This edge function makes sure the download process goes smoothly for the tracker.
